# .trivyignore — documented exceptions to the Trivy gate (PETPLAT-105)
#
# The build-push workflow fails on any CRITICAL vulnerability it finds. This
# file is the escape hatch, and it is deliberately the ONLY one: there is no
# `continue-on-error` on the scan step, so the single way to let a CRITICAL
# through is to name it here, in a commit, with a reason.
#
# That is the whole design. A gate with a second, quieter bypass is not a gate,
# and an exception that lives only in someone's memory is not an exception —
# it is a vulnerability nobody has agreed to accept yet.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Format
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# One entry per line. Trivy matches by vulnerability ID, and the ID may be
# followed by an optional free-text comment after `#`:
#
#   CVE-2024-12345        # shipped in a base image layer we do not control
#
# Trivy also accepts a `pkg:...` or `path:...` qualifier on the same line to
# narrow an exception to one package or one file, which is worth using whenever
# the ID is not itself the whole story:
#
#   CVE-2024-12345   pkg:jar/org.example/thing@1.2.3
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What every entry MUST carry in this repository
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# An entry is a decision to ship a known-vulnerable artifact, so the commit
# that adds one has to record the four things a reviewer needs:
#
#   1. Why it cannot be fixed right now — no upstream patch, or the patch is
#      blocked behind a major version we are not taking yet. "It was noisy" is
#      not a reason.
#   2. What it actually exposes here. A CRITICAL in a code path this service
#      never executes is a different risk from one on the request path, and
#      the difference is the whole justification.
#   3. Who accepted it, by ROLE rather than by name, and on what date. The
#      exception needs an owner for the day it is questioned.
#   4. When it gets looked at again. An exception with no review date becomes
#      permanent by default, which is how allowlists rot.
#
# Put that in the commit message and reference the ticket. Example:
#
#   CVE-2024-12345   # no upstream fix; unreachable in this service (see below)
#   #   exposure:  the vulnerable class is only loaded by the batch profile,
#   #              which no petclinic service activates
#   #   owner:     platform engineer, 2026-10-11
#   #   revisit:   2026-12-11, or when spring-boot 3.x.y lands
#   #   ticket:    PETPLAT-105
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Expiry is not enforced by Trivy here
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Trivy will honour an entry forever. The revisit date above is a promise, not
# a control, and nothing in this pipeline fails when it passes. If the list
# grows past a handful of entries, move it to `.trivyignore.yaml`, which
# supports an `expired_at` field that Trivy does enforce, and the date stops
# depending on anyone remembering it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Current exceptions
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# None. Every CRITICAL reported by the gate is currently expected to fail the
# build. This is the state to defend: the first entry here should be a decision
# someone made deliberately, not the first time a red build was inconvenient.
